artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $V = \bigoplus_{n\ge 0} V_{(n)}$ be a simple, $\mathbb{N}$-graded vertex operator algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ such that:
(i) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$;
(ii) $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n<0$;
(iii) $V$ is isomorphic to its contragredient module $V'$;
(iv) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e.\ $\dim V/C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V) = \mathrm{span}\{u_{-2}v : u,v\in V\}$.

Let $\mathcal{C}_V$ denote the braided tensor category of grading-restricted generalized $V$-modules (whose objects are finitely generated $V$-modules on which $L(0)$ acts locally finitely with grading bounded below and finite-dimensional generalized eigenspaces).

In the nonsemisimple setting, where $\mathcal{C}_V$ is in general not semisimple (as relevant to logarithmic conformal field theories):

(a) Formulate a definition of "modular tensor category" appropriate to the nonsemisimple (finite, possibly non-rigid-friendly, factorizable ribbon) situation that generalizes the classical (semisimple) Moore–Seiberg notion.

(b) Prove that for every $V$ as above, the category $\mathcal{C}_V$ carries the structure of such a nonsemisimple modular tensor category.

(c) Show that from any such nonsemisimple modular tensor category one can construct a $3$-dimensional topological quantum field theory yielding invariants of (framed/decorated) knots and closed oriented $3$-manifolds, generalizing the Reshetikhin–Turaev construction.
